    ('solar_visualization_shells.py', 'L-411 Termination Shock text: 75-100, Voyager 2, speeds', '# Source: constants_new.py TERMINATION_SHOCK_AU=94; Stone et al. (2005) Science -- Voyager 1 at 94 AU, Voyager 2 at 84 AU\ntermination_shock_info = (\n            "Solar Wind: Termination Shock:<br><br>"\n\n            "The Solar Wind Termination Shock extends from about 75 to 100 AU.<br><br>"\n\n            "The Termination Shock is the region where the solar wind slows down from<br>"\n            "supersonic to subsonic speeds due to interaction with the interstellar<br>"\n            "medium. The kinetic energy transfers into heat, increasing abruptly.<br><br>"\n\n            f"Voyager 1 encountered the Termination Shock at {_TERMINATION_SHOCK} AU, while Voyager 2 at 84 AU.<br>"\n            "After the Termination Shock the speeds slow down to ~100 to 200 km/s."\n', '# Source: Stone et al. (2005), Science 309:2017 -- Voyager 1\'s crossing;\n# Source+: NASA SVS 20132 (2008) -- Voyager 2\'s; Treumann and Jaroschek,\n# Source+: arXiv:0807.4170 -- the 80-100 AU range; Li, Wang and Richardson\n# Source+: (2008), GRL 35:L19107 -- Voyager 2\'s speeds. All print from\n# Source+: their rows in constants_new.py (L-411).\ntermination_shock_info = (\n            "Solar Wind: Termination Shock:<br><br>"\n\n            f"The Solar Wind Termination Shock lies about {row_text(\'TERMINATION_SHOCK_RANGE_LOW_AU\')} to {row_text(\'TERMINATION_SHOCK_RANGE_HIGH_AU\')} AU from the Sun (Treumann and Jaroschek 2008).<br><br>"\n\n            "The Termination Shock is the region where the solar wind slows down from<br>"\n            "supersonic to subsonic speeds due to interaction with the interstellar<br>"\n            "medium. The kinetic energy transfers into heat, increasing abruptly.<br><br>"\n\n            f"Voyager 1 encountered the Termination Shock at {_TERMINATION_SHOCK} AU, and Voyager 2 at {row_text(\'VOYAGER2_TERMINATION_SHOCK_AU\')} AU (NASA).<br>"\n            f"Voyager 2 measured the wind slowing from about {row_text(\'VOYAGER2_TS_UPSTREAM_SPEED_KM_S\')} to about {row_text(\'VOYAGER2_TS_DOWNSTREAM_SPEED_KM_S\')} km/s as it crossed (Li et al. 2008)."\n', 1),
    ('solar_visualization_shells.py', 'L-411 chromosphere radius line: the AU and depth from rows', '    f"  (~{SOLAR_RADIUS_AU:.5f} - {CHROMOSPHERE_PHYSICAL_RADII * SOLAR_RADIUS_AU:.5f} AU).<br>"\n    f"  The chromosphere is a skin about {CHROMOSPHERE_PHYSICAL_KM:,.0f} km deep on a star "\n', '    f"  ({row_text(\'SUN_RADIUS_KM\', \'au\')} to {row_text(\'CHROMOSPHERE_TOP_KM\', \'au\')} AU).<br>"\n    f"  The chromosphere is a skin about {row_text(\'CHROMOSPHERE_PHYSICAL_KM\', grouping=True)} km deep on a star "\n', 1),
    ('solar_visualization_shells.py', 'L-411 Photosphere: its thickness from its row', '            "* The photosphere is the visible surface of the Sun. It\'s a relatively thin layer, only about 500 kilometers thick.<br>" \n', '            f"* The photosphere is the visible surface of the Sun. It\'s a relatively thin layer, only about {row_text(\'PHOTOSPHERE_THICKNESS_KM\')} kilometers thick.<br>" \n', 2),
    ('exact_rows_report.py', 'L-371 the Sun pointers that are read only to draw', "    'position_accuracy':\n        ('gallery/solar_system_figures.js', 'sourcePlaceKm', 'node.value'),\n}\n", "    'position_accuracy':\n        ('gallery/solar_system_figures.js', 'sourcePlaceKm', 'node.value'),\n    # L-371 (2026-10-17): the Sun room's exact rows read only to draw.\n    # The Roche limit is drawn at its drawn_radius and reports `radius`;\n    # the galactic pole turns the tide and the galactic plane; the\n    # plane's ring radius sizes the ring and is never printed.\n    'solar_atmosphere/roche_limit/drawn_radius':\n        ('gallery/feature_renderers.js', 'renderShellSet',\n         'measuredRadiusAu(cfg.drawn_radius'),\n    'oort_cloud/galactic_tide/galactic_pole/ra':\n        ('gallery/feature_renderers.js', 'renderOortShape', 'measured(gp.ra'),\n    'oort_cloud/galactic_tide/galactic_pole/dec':\n        ('gallery/feature_renderers.js', 'renderOortShape', 'measured(gp.dec'),\n    'oort_cloud/galactic_plane/ring_radius':\n        ('gallery/feature_renderers.js', 'renderGalacticPlane',\n         'measuredAu(cfg.ring_radius'),\n    'oort_cloud/galactic_plane/galactic_pole/ra':\n        ('gallery/feature_renderers.js', 'renderGalacticPlane',\n         'measured(gp.ra'),\n    'oort_cloud/galactic_plane/galactic_pole/dec':\n        ('gallery/feature_renderers.js', 'renderGalacticPlane',\n         'measured(gp.dec'),\n}\n", 1),
    ('exact_rows_report.py', "L-371 the Sun pointers that are printed; Earth's stale low-orbit entry goes", "    'earth_orbital_zones/leo_inner/altitude':\n        ('gallery/feature_renderers.js', 'renderShellSet', 'km.altitudeKm'),\n    'earth_orbital_zones/leo_outer/altitude':\n        ('gallery/feature_renderers.js', 'renderShellSet', 'km.altitudeKm'),\n}\n", '    # L-371 (2026-10-17): leo_inner\'s altitude left this table. Its row,\n    # EARTH_LEO_LOWER_ALTITUDE_KM, became ESA\'s measured 180 km on\n    # 2026-10-17 (L-330) and is no longer exact, so no exact pointer\n    # reached the entry and it read BROKEN.\n    \'earth_orbital_zones/leo_outer/altitude\':\n        (\'gallery/feature_renderers.js\', \'renderShellSet\', \'km.altitudeKm\'),\n    # L-371 (2026-10-17): the Sun room\'s exact rows, each printed by its\n    # served count -- a shell\'s radius in solar radii, a far shell\'s in\n    # AU, the streamer belt\'s cusp, the Oort shapes\' edges.\n    \'solar_atmosphere/streamer_belt/cusp_radius\':\n        (\'gallery/feature_renderers.js\', \'renderStreamerBand\',\n         \'servedText(cfg.cusp_radius)\'),\n    \'solar_atmosphere/inner_corona\':\n        (\'gallery/feature_renderers.js\', \'renderShellSet\',\n         \'fmtServed(cfg.radius.value, sunF\'),\n    \'solar_atmosphere/outer_corona\':\n        (\'gallery/feature_renderers.js\', \'renderShellSet\',\n         \'fmtServed(cfg.radius.value, sunF\'),\n    \'oort_cloud/inner_oort_limit\':\n        (\'gallery/feature_renderers.js\', \'renderShellSet\',\n         \'servedText(cfg.radius) + " AU\'),\n    \'oort_cloud/outer_oort\':\n        (\'gallery/feature_renderers.js\', \'renderShellSet\',\n         \'servedText(cfg.radius) + " AU\'),\n    \'oort_cloud/hills_cloud_torus/inner_radius\':\n        (\'gallery/feature_renderers.js\', \'renderOortShape\',\n         \'fmtAuServed(cfg.inner_radius)\'),\n    \'oort_cloud/outer_oort_clumpy/outer_radius\':\n        (\'gallery/feature_renderers.js\', \'bandLine\',\n         \'fmtAuServed(cfg.outer_radius)\'),\n    \'oort_cloud/galactic_tide/outer_radius\':\n        (\'gallery/feature_renderers.js\', \'bandLine\',\n         \'fmtAuServed(cfg.outer_radius)\'),\n}\n', 1),
    ('exact_rows_report.py', 'L-371 the served-count printers count as print lines', "    r'fmtServed\\(|_fmtServed\\(|sigFigures\\(|kmAndAu\\(|fmtKm\\(|'\n    r'\\.toFixed\\(|\\.toPrecision\\(|toLocaleString\\(')\n", "    r'fmtServed\\(|_fmtServed\\(|sigFigures\\(|kmAndAu\\(|fmtKm\\(|'\n    r'\\.toFixed\\(|\\.toPrecision\\(|toLocaleString\\(|'\n    # L-371 (2026-10-17): the helpers that print a served node at its\n    # served count. Without them a radius printed by servedText() was\n    # not a print line, and its pointer could not be followed.\n    r'servedText\\(|fmtAuServed\\(|kmAndAuServed\\(|inText\\(')\n", 1),
    ('exact_rows_report.py', 'L-371 credit line', 'check could not see it -- found when the patched tree reported fewer\nprinted rows than before.)\n"""\n', 'check could not see it -- found when the patched tree reported fewer\nprinted rows than before.)\nModule updated: October 17, 2026 with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5\n(L-371 Gap 1: the Sun room\'s fourteen pointers to exact rows followed\n-- eight printed, six read only to draw -- and the gallery\'s\nserved-count printers counted as print lines. leo_inner\'s altitude\nentry removed: its row is measured since L-330.)\n"""\n', 1),
    ('exact_rows_report.py', 'L-371 the count is read from the slot the pointer governs', "        node = served_node(config, path)\n                got = node.get('prints') if isinstance(node, dict) else None\n", "        node = served_slot(served_node(config, path))\n                got = node.get('prints') if isinstance(node, dict) else None\n", 1),
    ('exact_rows_report.py', "L-371 served_slot, the mirror's rule", 'def read_lines(path):\n', 'def served_slot(node):\n    """The slot a pointer governs, by the mirror\'s rule\n    (tools/mirror_constants.py, the slot of an entry): the entry itself\n    where it holds a value, otherwise its member that does -- a whole\n    shell\'s `radius`. L-371 (2026-10-17): until then the count was read\n    from the entry, so a shell whose pointer sits on the feature read\n    as served no count while its radius carried one."""\n    if not isinstance(node, dict) or \'value\' in node:\n        return node\n    for child in node.values():\n        if isinstance(child, dict) and \'value\' in child:\n            return child\n    return None\n\n\ndef read_lines(path):\n', 1),
    ('exact_rows_report.py', 'L-371 the photosphere, printed as one solar radius', "    'solar_atmosphere/inner_corona':\n", "    'sun_structures/photosphere':\n        ('gallery/feature_renderers.js', 'renderShellSet',\n         'fmtServed(cfg.radius.value, sunF'),\n    'solar_atmosphere/inner_corona':\n", 1),
    ('exact_rows_report.py', "L-371 a slot in another unit is compared with the row's count in that unit", "            want = constants_rows.prints_of(n, HERE)\n            for f, l, code, path in g_sites.get(n, []):\n                node = served_slot(served_node(config, path))\n                got = node.get('prints') if isinstance(node, dict) else None\n", "            own = constants_rows.prints_of(n, HERE)\n            for f, l, code, path in g_sites.get(n, []):\n                node = served_slot(served_node(config, path))\n                got = node.get('prints') if isinstance(node, dict) else None\n                # L-371 (2026-10-17): a slot drawn in another unit than\n                # its row -- the photosphere, one solar radius, from the\n                # kilometre row -- is written from the row's value in that\n                # unit, so its count is the row's count there, as the\n                # orrery's export serves it, not the row's own.\n                want = own\n                unit = node.get('unit') if isinstance(node, dict) else None\n                served = export_rows.get(n) or {}\n                if unit and served.get('unit') and \\\n                        unit.lower() != served['unit'].lower():\n                    want = ((served.get('in') or {}).get(unit.lower())\n                            or {}).get('prints')\n", 1),
    ('exact_rows_report.py', "L-371 the orrery's export, read beside the config", '        pointers = config_pointers(config, names)\n', "        pointers = config_pointers(config, names)\n    export_rows = {}\n    export_path = os.path.join(HERE, 'data', 'constants_export.json')\n    if os.path.exists(export_path):\n        import json\n        with open(export_path, 'r', encoding='utf-8') as handle:\n            export_rows = json.load(handle).get('rows', {})\n    if gallery_read:\n", 1),
    ('constants_change_report.py', 'L-411 a typed row that becomes derived is named, not unreadable (print)', "    for name, before in unreadable:\n        print('  %-30s STILL PRESENT -- value unreadable' % name)\n", "    for name, before in now_derived:\n        print('  %-30s NOW DERIVED (was %s)' % (name, before))\n        print('      Its typed value became an expression over other')\n        print('      rows; the derived line below names its parents.')\n        print()\n\n    for name, before in unreadable:\n        print('  %-30s STILL PRESENT -- value unreadable' % name)\n", 1),
    ('constants_change_report.py', 'L-411 a typed row that becomes derived is named, not unreadable (sort)', '    unreadable = []\n    if present is None:\n', "    unreadable = []\n    # L-411 (2026-10-17): a typed row rewritten as an expression over\n    # other rows leaves the diff's literal list and comes back as a\n    # derived line, which read_changes() parses. It is neither removed\n    # nor unreadable, so it is named as such. Found when\n    # PARKER_CLOSEST_RADII, typed 9.86, became a conversion of\n    # PARKER_CLOSEST_KM.\n    became = set(n for n, _expr, _parents in derived_added)\n    now_derived = [(n, b) for n, b in removed if n in became]\n    removed = [(n, b) for n, b in removed if n not in became]\n    if present is None:\n", 1),
    ('constants_change_report.py', 'L-411 credit line', '    still assigns is reported unreadable, not removed).\n"""\n', '    still assigns is reported unreadable, not removed).\nModule updated: October 17, 2026 with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5\n    (L-411: a typed row rewritten as an expression over other rows is\n    reported NOW DERIVED, with its old value, instead of unreadable;\n    found when PARKER_CLOSEST_RADII became a conversion).\n"""\n', 1),
    ('constants_new.py', 'credit line', 'the only spacecraft sheets of the 26 the orrery draws that state one.\nNo value changes.)\n"""\n', 'the only spacecraft sheets of the 26 the orrery draws that state one.\nNo value changes.)\nModule updated: October 17, 2026 with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5\n(the Sun\'s numbers, L-228, L-411, L-386 and L-371: the Alfven\nsurface\'s usual range as two rows and the coronagraph floor as a row,\nfrom Cranmer et al. (2023) and DeForest, Howard and McComas (2014); the\ncore\'s radius and the photosphere\'s thickness and temperature from\nHathaway\'s NASA/Marshall pages, and the radiative zone\'s edge as a\nmeasured row from Delahaye and Pinsonneault; Parker Solar Probe\'s\nclosest approach as its altitude, its distance from the centre derived;\nthe termination shock\'s range (Treumann and Jaroschek), Voyager 2\'s\ncrossing (NASA SVS) and the wind\'s speed either side (Li, Wang and\nRichardson); the chromosphere\'s depth re-homed to an open source.\nCORE_AU, RADIATIVE_ZONE_AU, SOLAR_RADIUS_AU and PARKER_CLOSEST_RADII\nare conversions of their rows.)\n"""\n', 1),
    ('solar_visualization_shells.py', 'credit line', "Domain: orrery\n\nModule updated: October 11, 2026 with Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5\n(L-421, the website half: the Hills cloud draws\n", "Domain: orrery\n\nModule updated: October 17, 2026 with Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5\n(the Sun's numbers, L-228 and L-411: the hovers print the Alfven\nsurface's usual range, the coronagraph floor, Parker Solar Probe's\nclosest approach, the inner corona, the helmets, the core, radiative\nzone and photosphere edges, the photosphere's thickness, the\nchromosphere's line and the termination shock's range, crossing and\nwind speeds from their rows, each with a citation the visitor sees.\nSedna's farthest point is worked out from the period record the\norrery fetched, measured from the solar system's centre of mass. The\npole-and-streamer ranges, which had the shape backwards, are gone, and\nthe Voyagers' line says both are past the heliopause. Words approved\nby Tony.)\nModule updated: October 11, 2026 with Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5\n(L-421, the website half: the Hills cloud draws\n", 1),
    ('skills/provenance-discipline/SKILL.md', 'version 2.31', "Skill version: 2.30 | 2026-10-17, with Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5, at\npalomas_orrery @ a73bc24a. v2.30 (L-452 with L-450, Earth's round 1)\n", 'Skill version: 2.31 | 2026-10-17, with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5, at\npalomas_orrery @ 6f4fc9b0. v2.31 (L-411, the Sun\'s numbers) adds one\nparagraph to The Unit Field: a temperature is stored in kelvin, a\nsource in degrees Celsius is converted exactly and the row says so,\nand the words a visitor reads give kelvin. Tony, 2026-10-17: "don\'t\nwe standardize on degrees Kelvin? the conversion is routine", and "we\nshould add this to the skill."\nEarlier: 2.30 | 2026-10-17, with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5, at\npalomas_orrery @ a73bc24a. v2.30 (L-452 with L-450, Earth\'s round 1)\n', 1),
    ('skills/provenance-discipline/SKILL.md', 'the 2.27 entry moves down', "Earlier: 2.27 | 2026-10-08, with Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5, at\npalomas_orrery @ b0b3df82. v2.27 (L-418) splits the skill, and no rule is\nreworded. The relay procedure, the Review-Repair Protocol, is now the\nskill provenance-cross-check. Rules 1 to 8 of the figure count moved to\nreferences/figures.md and the field notes to references/field-notes.md,\neach opened when its pointer says; the withdrawn derived-row rule moved\nto documentation/SKILL_HISTORIES.md. Every moved section is word for\nword apart from the edits the build patch lists. The file opens with a\nread plan, written by skills_index.py, because one read does not show\nit whole: the design session's viewer showed 16,000 characters, from\nthe start and the end, and this session's reader showed lines 1 to\n1,106 and said it had stopped (2026-10-08). Riding this version: the\nrange rule's examples name rows that exist, and a patch predicts the\nscanner's change rather than its total (both L-371); Rule 1 names the\nconversion marker and Rule 8 records the widening as built (L-390); and\nthe examples in The Status Line and Report to the Figures You Have name\nrows as they now stand.\nOlder entries are in documentation/SKILL_HISTORIES.md, moved there\non 2026-10-05 and 2026-10-08 (L-418), 2026-10-09 (L-414),\n2026-10-13 (L-437) and 2026-10-17 (L-452).\n", 'Older entries are in documentation/SKILL_HISTORIES.md, moved there\non 2026-10-05 and 2026-10-08 (L-418), 2026-10-09 (L-414),\n2026-10-13 (L-437) and 2026-10-17 (L-452 and L-411).\n', 1),
    ('skills/provenance-discipline/SKILL.md', 'The Unit Field: temperatures in kelvin', "(Tony's rulings, 2026-09-11 and 2026-09-14, recorded on L-322 and until\nthis version living only there. In engineering he has always used\nunits, not literals.)\n\n", '(Tony\'s rulings, 2026-09-11 and 2026-09-14, recorded on L-322 and until\nthis version living only there. In engineering he has always used\nunits, not literals.)\n\n**A temperature is stored in kelvin, and a visitor reads kelvin.** `k`\nis the store\'s one temperature token. A source that prints degrees\nCelsius is converted exactly, by adding 273.15: the row\'s `# Source:`\nquotes the figure as the source prints it, a `# Note:` says it was\nconverted, and the row keeps the source\'s place, so 5,400 degrees\nCelsius, known to the hundred, is stored as 5,700. At millions of\ndegrees the addition changes no printed figure. The words a visitor\nreads give kelvin too, on both sites: a Celsius or Fahrenheit figure\ntyped into them is a typed number like any other, sourced into a row\nor removed. (Tony, 2026-10-17, L-411: "don\'t we standardize on\ndegrees Kelvin? the conversion is routine", and "we should add this\nto the skill." The Sun\'s words were converted that evening; Earth\'s\nstill say Celsius, on L-455.)\n\n', 1),
    ('documentation/SKILL_HISTORIES.md', 'provenance-discipline 2.27 moved down', '(Moved down 2026-10-17, when 2.30 made a fourth entry.)\n\n## interactive-exhibit\n', "(Moved down 2026-10-17, when 2.30 made a fourth entry.)\n\nEarlier: 2.27 | 2026-10-08, with Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5, at\npalomas_orrery @ b0b3df82. v2.27 (L-418) splits the skill, and no rule is\nreworded. The relay procedure, the Review-Repair Protocol, is now the\nskill provenance-cross-check. Rules 1 to 8 of the figure count moved to\nreferences/figures.md and the field notes to references/field-notes.md,\neach opened when its pointer says; the withdrawn derived-row rule moved\nto documentation/SKILL_HISTORIES.md. Every moved section is word for\nword apart from the edits the build patch lists. The file opens with a\nread plan, written by skills_index.py, because one read does not show\nit whole: the design session's viewer showed 16,000 characters, from\nthe start and the end, and this session's reader showed lines 1 to\n1,106 and said it had stopped (2026-10-08). Riding this version: the\nrange rule's examples name rows that exist, and a patch predicts the\nscanner's change rather than its total (both L-371); Rule 1 names the\nconversion marker and Rule 8 records the widening as built (L-390); and\nthe examples in The Status Line and Report to the Figures You Have name\nrows as they now stand.\n(Moved down 2026-10-17, when 2.31 made a fourth entry.)\n\n## interactive-exhibit\n", 1),
    ('PROJECT_INSTRUCTIONS.md', 'header stamp', 'Tony Quintanilla, PE | Claude | v3.102 | October 17, 2026\n\nCut from a73bc24a at https://github.com/tonylquintanilla/palomas_orrery\n', 'Tony Quintanilla, PE | Claude | v3.103 | October 17, 2026\n\nCut from 6f4fc9b0 at https://github.com/tonylquintanilla/palomas_orrery\n', 1),
    ('PROJECT_INSTRUCTIONS.md', 'v3.103 entry', 'that file. An entry lives in exactly one place, never both.\n\nv3.102 (October 17, 2026): No rule changed in this document. THREE\n', 'that file. An entry lives in exactly one place, never both.\n\nv3.103 (October 17, 2026): No rule changed in this document. ONE\nskill bump, one version (L-411, in the Sun\'s numbers build, L-412):\nprovenance-discipline 2.30 -> 2.31. A TEMPERATURE IS IN KELVIN.\n\nWHAT PROMPTED IT. The first wording list for the Sun\'s numbers gave\nthe interior\'s temperatures in degrees Celsius, as NASA/Marshall\nprints them. Tony, 2026-10-17: "don\'t we standardize on degrees\nKelvin? the conversion is routine." Then: "we should add this to the\nskill."\n\nWHAT CHANGED. provenance-discipline\'s The Unit Field gains one\nparagraph: a temperature is stored in kelvin, the store\'s one\ntemperature token; a source in degrees Celsius is converted exactly,\nadding 273.15, the row saying so and keeping the source\'s place; the\nwords a visitor reads give kelvin, and a Celsius or Fahrenheit figure\ntyped into them is a typed number like any other. Its v2.27 entry\nmoved to documentation/SKILL_HISTORIES.md, by the three-entry rule.\n\nTHE OBLIGATION TRAVELS. A reinstall during a session is not visible to\nthat session. The next session confirms its loaded copy reads\nprovenance-discipline 2.31 before provenance work. This session\nconfirmed its loaded copies read provenance-discipline 2.30,\ninteractive-exhibit 1.17 and gallery-cache-builder 1.11 (discharging\nv3.102\'s obligation), horizons-orbital-mechanics 1.8 (discharging\nv3.99\'s), ledger-and-session-records 1.21, safe-file-editing 1.13\nand agentic-pre-test 1.3, as the manifest expected.\n\nThe header stamp and the SHA anchor move with this entry.\n\nVersion history: v3.100 moves down to\ndocumentation/PROJECT_INSTRUCTIONS_HISTORY.md PART 1 to keep three\nresident.\n\nv3.102 (October 17, 2026): No rule changed in this document. THREE\n', 1),
    ('PROJECT_INSTRUCTIONS.md', 'v3.100 moves down', 'v3.100 (October 16, 2026): No rule changed in this document. ONE\nskill bump, one version (L-446, with L-380 and L-388):\ngallery-cache-builder 1.9 -> 1.10. THE WEBSITE\'S DAILY RUN PULLS\nBEFORE IT BUILDS.\n\nWHAT PROMPTED IT. Tony, 2026-10-15, after L-444\'s first live run:\n"Wait, shouldn\'t the horizons check come before the cache build?" The\ncheck already runs in the orrery, before the export; what came after\nthe build was the website\'s pull of that export, so each build read\nthe previous run\'s pull. Ruled the same day: the pulls run before the\nbuilder. At the build\'s start Tony chose option A for a pull that\ncannot reach the orrery: the Daily Run stops before the builder.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. gallery-cache-builder rewrites The Daily Run\'s Steps:\nfour steps, the pulls (gallery_maintenance_run.py --pull, reading the\none pull list in its GENERATORS) before the builder; a pull that\ncannot fetch fails by name and its mirror does not run; the Daily Run\nstops there; "Pull failures" tests those branches every run. Its\ndescription and trigger name the Daily Run\'s order. Its v1.8 entry\nmoved to documentation/SKILL_HISTORIES.md, by the three-entry rule.\nBuilt at 77245ea8; rebuilt on 54c27d7 after L-445\'s build (v3.99)\nlanded first, so this entry is v3.100.\n\nTHE OBLIGATION TRAVELS. A reinstall during a session is not visible to\nthat session. The next session confirms its loaded copy reads\ngallery-cache-builder 1.10 before work that fires it. This session\nconfirmed its loaded copies read gallery-cache-builder 1.9,\nsafe-file-editing 1.13, agentic-pre-test 1.3 and\nledger-and-session-records 1.21, as the manifest expected. v3.99\'s\nobligation, horizons-orbital-mechanics 1.8, still travels: no work\nthis session fired that skill.\n\nThe header stamp and the SHA anchor move with this entry.\n\nVersion history: v3.97 moves down to\ndocumentation/PROJECT_INSTRUCTIONS_HISTORY.md PART 1 to keep three\nresident.\n\n', '', 1),
    ('documentation/PROJECT_INSTRUCTIONS_HISTORY.md', 'v3.100 moved down', '(Moved down from the resident protocol on 2026-10-17 when\nv3.102 made a fourth entry.)\n\n================================================================\nPART 2 -- LESSONS REMOVED FROM THE PROTOCOL AT v3.37\n', '(Moved down from the resident protocol on 2026-10-17 when\nv3.102 made a fourth entry.)\n\nv3.100 (October 16, 2026): No rule changed in this document. ONE\nskill bump, one version (L-446, with L-380 and L-388):\ngallery-cache-builder 1.9 -> 1.10. THE WEBSITE\'S DAILY RUN PULLS\nBEFORE IT BUILDS.\n\nWHAT PROMPTED IT. Tony, 2026-10-15, after L-444\'s first live run:\n"Wait, shouldn\'t the horizons check come before the cache build?" The\ncheck already runs in the orrery, before the export; what came after\nthe build was the website\'s pull of that export, so each build read\nthe previous run\'s pull. Ruled the same day: the pulls run before the\nbuilder. At the build\'s start Tony chose option A for a pull that\ncannot reach the orrery: the Daily Run stops before the builder.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. gallery-cache-builder rewrites The Daily Run\'s Steps:\nfour steps, the pulls (gallery_maintenance_run.py --pull, reading the\none pull list in its GENERATORS) before the builder; a pull that\ncannot fetch fails by name and its mirror does not run; the Daily Run\nstops there; "Pull failures" tests those branches every run. Its\ndescription and trigger name the Daily Run\'s order. Its v1.8 entry\nmoved to documentation/SKILL_HISTORIES.md, by the three-entry rule.\nBuilt at 77245ea8; rebuilt on 54c27d7 after L-445\'s build (v3.99)\nlanded first, so this entry is v3.100.\n\nTHE OBLIGATION TRAVELS. A reinstall during a session is not visible to\nthat session. The next session confirms its loaded copy reads\ngallery-cache-builder 1.10 before work that fires it. This session\nconfirmed its loaded copies read gallery-cache-builder 1.9,\nsafe-file-editing 1.13, agentic-pre-test 1.3 and\nledger-and-session-records 1.21, as the manifest expected. v3.99\'s\nobligation, horizons-orbital-mechanics 1.8, still travels: no work\nthis session fired that skill.\n\nThe header stamp and the SHA anchor move with this entry.\n\nVersion history: v3.97 moves down to\ndocumentation/PROJECT_INSTRUCTIONS_HISTORY.md PART 1 to keep three\nresident.\n\n(Moved down from the resident protocol on 2026-10-17 when\nv3.103 made a fourth entry.)\n\n================================================================\nPART 2 -- LESSONS REMOVED FROM THE PROTOCOL AT v3.37\n', 1),
    ('LEDGER_CONSOLIDATED.md', 'header stamp', "(two notes from Earth's round 1: a rule owed on L-351, a locator owed\non L-314), built on bc25fdc8 and gallery 44a6225c.\nReview and RICE update Tony 6-21-2026\n", "(two notes from Earth's round 1: a rule owed on L-351, a locator owed\non L-314), built on bc25fdc8 and gallery 44a6225c.\nModule updated: October 17, 2026 with Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5\n(the Sun's numbers, L-412's first half: L-228, L-386 and L-371 done;\nL-411's groups W, S, D and N done, its bulk left on it; L-455 the\nnumbers' last item; provenance-discipline 2.31, temperatures in\nkelvin), built on 6f4fc9b0 and gallery da442bf2.\nReview and RICE update Tony 6-21-2026\n", 1),
    ('LEDGER_CONSOLIDATED.md', 'L-228 meta', '<!-- L:228 status:OPEN upd:2026-10-08 section:A flag: rice: -->', '<!-- L:228 status:DONE upd:2026-10-17 section:A flag: rice: -->', 1),
    ('LEDGER_CONSOLIDATED.md', 'L-228 done', '  unknown until somebody reads it. **Tony-action (decide):** confirm\n  or redirect.\n- **Ref:** `solar_visualization_shells.py` (`outer_corona_info_hover`,\n', '  unknown until somebody reads it. **Tony-action (decide):** confirm\n  or redirect.\n- **2026-10-17, DONE** (Claude Opus 5.5, the Sun\'s numbers session;\n  Tony approved the wording list at 21:25, after asking that it meet\n  the five principles of What a Visitor Reads). Cranmer et al. (2007)\n  does not state these ranges: its arXiv copy, read up to Section 8,\n  gives no Alfven radius. The same first author\'s open review,\n  Cranmer et al. (2023), does: "between about 10 and 20 solar radii"\n  most of the time (abstract), now ALFVEN_SURFACE_RANGE_LOW_RADII and\n  _HIGH_RADII. Its sec. 3.1 says models often put the surface\n  farthest out above the polar coronal holes and closest in at the\n  streamer tops: the hovers had it the other way round, and so did the\n  website\'s Alfven words, corrected with L-411. The 12 and 15 were\n  DeForest, Howard and McComas (2014)\'s "at least" floors; the 15,\n  the coronagraph\'s field of view, is ALFVEN_SURFACE_STREAMER_FLOOR_RADII.\n  The three typed ranges are gone; each changed line carries\n  "(Cranmer et al. 2023)". L-229\'s loose end is not in the review\n  either: the streamer belt\'s orientation stays a declared drawing\n  choice. The RICE question above lapsed with the rule of 2026-10-07.\n- **Ref:** `solar_visualization_shells.py` (`outer_corona_info_hover`,\n', 1),
    ('LEDGER_CONSOLIDATED.md', 'L-411 meta', '<!-- L:411 status:OPEN upd:2026-10-04 section:A flag: rice: -->', '<!-- L:411 status:OPEN upd:2026-10-17 section:A flag: rice: -->', 1),
    ('LEDGER_CONSOLIDATED.md', 'L-411 groups W, S, D and N done', "**Gap:** the Sun slice's fifth item (L-412), straight after L-228.\n", '- **2026-10-17, the first four groups done** (Claude Opus 5.5; Tony\'s\n  scope ruling and his approval of the wording list, revision 2, the\n  same evening). Discovery first, apart from remediation:\n  `documentation/DISCOVERY_L411_sun_typed_numbers_20261017.md` lists\n  every number typed into the Sun\'s visitor text, by kind and by\n  string: 340 digit runs in 40 orrery strings, about 170 claims, and\n  15 numbers in 9 website word fields. Done: W, the website\'s Sun\n  words, which print their numbers from rows or no longer type them;\n  S, the numbers the store already held, printed through it, which\n  corrected Parker\'s closest approach (9.85 solar radii from the\n  Sun\'s centre; the 8.8 was its height) and the MAPS sentence built\n  on it; D, the Voyagers\' heliopause line and the streamer belt\'s\n  ruling date; N, Voyager 2\'s 84 AU (NASA SVS), Sedna\'s farthest\n  point (937 AU, from the period record fetched around the solar\n  system\'s barycentre), the termination shock\'s 80 to 100 AU\n  (Treumann and Jaroschek 2008), the wind\'s speed across it (Li, Wang\n  and Richardson 2008) and the 15 solar radii floor. Removed for want\n  of a source: the core\'s "a third of its mass" and "the hottest\n  place in the solar system". Before and after:\n  `documentation/WORDS_L411_L386_sun_numbers_20261017.md`.\n- **Tony\'s two questions on revision 1:** kelvin, now a rule in\n  provenance-discipline 2.31; and "No source?" on three removals,\n  each of which had one. A search run once and stopped is not "no\n  source".\n- **Left on this item:** groups T (textbook figures: temperatures,\n  the wavelengths worked from them, the core\'s make-up, thicknesses,\n  speeds, the corona\'s extents, `hover_text_sun`, the Oort cloud\'s\n  counts), M (the comet MAPS\'s 2026 dates and distances,\n  observations that migrate to the store) and C (the comets that\n  survived inside the Roche limit); the streamer band\'s 7.25 degrees\n  (the solar pole, a dict with no rows); the Roche formula\'s typed\n  inputs; and the orrery photosphere\'s 5,500 K with the wavelength\n  worked from it, which move together. Tony, on the method:\n  "settle as much as possible by method rather than by judgement."\n**Gap:** groups T, M and C and the three leftovers above, one shell\nat a time. Not part of the Sun\'s numbers half, which ends with L-455.\n', 1),
